## ===========================================================================
## THE MARKET BACKDROPS — what is everywhere in Grubber's, and the smell and
## sound of the place
##
## Book 2, Part 19 (`story.ni:3324-3617`): the things the source spreads
## across the whole market as BACKDROPS — the crowd of shoppers, the pale
## stone wall around the perimeter, the stalls as a generic thing, the
## canvas awnings, the support wires, and Grubber's Market itself as a
## noun — plus the source's answers to a bare `smell` and `listen`, which
## in 2009 fell through to the market backdrop from every room that had no
## line of its own. The Alley's garbage rides along because the alley's
## own smell answer is its line (`story.ni:1545-1557`). Every finished line
## is Gentry's, carried verbatim; nothing here is placeholder.
##
## HOW A BACKDROP IS BUILT. Chord places a thing in one room, and has no
## backdrop kind — so each backdrop is ONE scenery entity that FOLLOWS the
## player: a `when Jack moves` clause moves it into her room whenever she
## arrives somewhere the backdrop belongs (which is what Inform's backdrop
## machinery does under the hood). The clause fires on a walked arrival and
## on an authorial `move` alike (the theft ejects, the blocked-stall bounce,
## the slide's landing), so the backdrop is never a room behind. Where she
## goes somewhere it does NOT belong — Commerce Street, Behind Fruit Stall,
## On the Wire — it stays in the last room it was in and is simply out of
## scope. Each backdrop starts in the Northwest Junction, where Jack does.
##
## WHERE EACH ONE BELONGS: the source's two regions are DECLARED below —
## the Open-Air Market with the Market Perimeter nested inside it, as
## `story.ni:3326-3330, 3455-3459` has them — and the named conditions test
## membership (`the player is in the Open-Air Market`), which is true in
## every member room, transitively through the nesting, and needs no
## `landing` line (GH #339, fixed 2026-08-30). The sets regions cannot
## carry — the whole of Grubber's including the alley, roof, tent and
## post; the awnings' and wires' reaches; the smell/sound partitions —
## stay as condition compositions over the region tests and room names.
##
## A BARE `smell` OR `listen` reaches no entity clause — there is no target
## to consult (`smelling.ts:38-40`), and a bare head on Jack (`on smelling`)
## fires on EVERY smell including the targeted ones (GH #340's collision is
## fixed — target-first, 2026-08-30 — but a bare head would still answer
## every unclaused targeted smell, which is not the location fallback the
## source wrote) — so the two are story actions with the bare grammar only (`sniffing`, `hearkening`; the shape `peering.chord`
## established and GH #317 records the seam of): `smell bread` still parses
## to the platform's smelling, a bare `smell` comes here. Every matching
## line in a story action's body fires, so the tables below are guarded to
## be exclusive; the last line of each is Gentry's own fallback
## (`story.ni:519, 526`) for the rooms with no answer. This also carries the
## three bare-verb lines earlier increments recorded as unreachable: the
## Leather and Candle stalls' smells (`story.ni:2557, 2795`), and the
## eavesdrop's alley and roof `listen` (`story.ni:1794, 1796`), each on its
## existing phrase key.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * The conspicuous shopper (`story.ni:3389-3453`) — a one-in-ten passer-by
##     with a printed name composed at random from nine adjectives and seven
##     varieties, and a sentence composed from three random parts. Chord
##     cannot compose a name or interpolate a phrase into a phrase (GH #303,
##     item 2); spelled out as whole sentences it would be several hundred,
##     which is a rewrite, not a carry. The change document lists the
##     one-in-ten roll as mechanism to keep ("The sweep"); it waits on the
##     platform.
##   * `x stall` at a stall examining a random one of its wares, and `steal
##     stall` stealing one (`story.ni:3480-3489`) — no random-entity draw and
##     no `try <action>` statement. `x stall` prints the stalls' own
##     description instead; `steal stall` falls to the platform's take.
##   * The source's `departing` rules ("There's only two ways out of
##     Grubber's…", `story.ni:3610-3616`) — dead code in 2009: no grammar
##     anywhere in the source reaches the action. Not carried; the lines are
##     kept aside in a comment at the end of this file.
##   * "crowd of people" as the shoppers' name in a which-do-you-mean
##     prompt, and the source's "does the player mean" preferences (the
##     mercenaries over the shoppers for "people"; the stall over the
##     shoppers for "merchants") — Chord has neither. `people` stays on the
##     shoppers as the source has it, so on the roof, where the group of
##     mercenaries also answers to it, `x people` asks which; `merchants`
##     is on the shoppers and not the stalls, so it never asks.
##   * The stall synonyms by location ("fruit" is the stall in the Fruit
##     Stall, "hats" in the Hat Stall…, `story.ni:3502-3511`) — no
##     conditional aliases; those words already name the rooms' wares.
##   * `climb wall` → "too high" is carried; `descend wall` → try going down
##     is not (no perimeter room has a down exit; nothing to reach).
##   * The stalls closed "for the night" — Chapter 1 has no night.
##   * The adjectives the source lets name the crowd (fat, gangly, pock
##     marked…) — they exist for the vanished conspicuous shopper.
##   * The shoppers' whole-crowd refusal ("The shopper roughly shoulders you
##     aside…") covers the physical verbs listed on the block; the source's
##     "doing something other than talking to or examining" has no
##     equivalent, so verbs outside the list fall to the platform's own line.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## WHERE THINGS ARE — the source's two regions and its per-backdrop lists,
## as named conditions (`story.ni:3326-3330, 3455-3459` and the backdrop
## declarations). `the Weapons Stall` and `the Candle Stall` are the rooms'
## own akas: a possessive name cannot stand in a condition (GH #336).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the Open-Air Market
  a region
  containing the Rope Stall, the Candle Stall, the Pottery Stall
  containing Outside the Silk Tent, the Market Perimeter

create the Market Perimeter
  a region
  containing the Fruit Stall, the Northwest Junction, the Grocery Stall
  containing the Eastern Junction, the Hat Stall, the Exotic Gems Stall
  containing the Weapons Stall, the Leather Stall, the Herb Stall

define condition open-air: the player is in the Open-Air Market

define condition market-perimeter: the player is in the Market Perimeter

define condition within-grubbers: open-air or the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Outer Market Roof or the player is in Inside the Silk Tent or the player is in the Base of the Center Post or the player is in the Top of the Post

define condition under-the-awnings: open-air or the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Outer Market Roof

define condition among-the-wires: under-the-awnings or the player is in the Base of the Center Post

## The rooms whose bare `smell` is the market's own — every room the market
## backdrop stands in that has no smell line of its own (the Alley, the
## Leather, Candle and Fruit stalls, the tent and the top have theirs).

## The ten rooms where a stallkeeper stands behind the goods.

define condition at-a-stall: the player is in the Grocery Stall or the player is in the Fruit Stall or the player is in the Hat Stall or the player is in the Leather Stall or the player is in the Weapons Stall or the player is in the Exotic Gems Stall or the player is in the Herb Stall or the player is in the Rope Stall or the player is in the Candle Stall or the player is in the Pottery Stall

define condition market-air: the player is in the Northwest Junction or the player is in the Grocery Stall or the player is in the Eastern Junction or the player is in the Hat Stall or the player is in the Weapons Stall or the player is in the Exotic Gems Stall or the player is in the Herb Stall or the player is in the Rope Stall or the player is in the Pottery Stall or the player is in Outside the Silk Tent or the player is in the Outer Market Roof or the player is in the Base of the Center Post

## The same for a bare `listen`: the tent and the top answer for
## themselves, and the alley and the roof answer for the eavesdrop while it
## is on.

define condition market-din: open-air or the player is in the Base of the Center Post

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SHOPPERS (`story.ni:3334-3388`)
##
## The crowd. In the five rooms away from the stalls — the roof, the alley,
## behind the tent, the top of the post, inside the tent — the source
## answers ANY handling of the crowd with where the crowd actually is, so
## every clause below opens with those four lines. In the market proper the
## description grows its mercenaries sentence while the pair is in the room,
## talking is refused twice over (`stopping`), and any laying-on of hands
## gets one of four shoves at random. A verb outside the covered set falls
## to the platform default, whose listening line does not agree in number
## ("The shoppers isn't making any sound." — GH #343).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the shoppers
  aka main crowd, crowd, crowds, people, marketfolk, market folk, marketgoers, market goers, townsfolk, townfolk, town folk, townspeople, town people, passers by, passersby, folk, merchants, buyers, shopper, customer, customers, others, hagglers, browsers, knot, knots
  scenery, plural
  in the Northwest Junction

  The crowd is thick with shoppers and merchants alike, all shouting at each
  other, shuffling and squeezing against each other to get a better look at
  the merchandise. Staying out of sight should be easy, as long as you keep
  moving.

  when Jack moves, while within-grubbers
    move the shoppers here
  end when

  on the player examining
    phrase shoppers-from-roof when the player is in the Outer Market Roof
    phrase shoppers-from-back when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post
    phrase shoppers-far-below when the player is in the Top of the Post
    phrase shoppers-outside-tent when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    phrase shoppers-wary-of-mercenaries when open-air and the wandering mercenaries is here
  end on

  on the player talking
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-no-chat
  end on

  on the player smelling
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    phrase shoppers-smell
  end on

  on the player listening
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    phrase shoppers-sound
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player touching
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player pulling
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player attacking
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player searching
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player climbing
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

  on the player entering
    refuse when the player is in the Outer Market Roof: shoppers-from-roof
    refuse when the player is in the Alley or the player is in the Base of the Center Post: shoppers-from-back
    refuse when the player is in the Top of the Post: shoppers-far-below
    refuse when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent: shoppers-outside-tent
    refuse shoppers-shove
  end on

define phrase shoppers-from-roof
  The main crowd is in the market proper, shopping amidst the stalls. Nobody
  ever climbs up here (except you).
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-from-back
  The main crowd is in the market proper, shopping amidst the stalls. Nobody
  comes back here.
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-far-below
  The crowd is far below you.
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-outside-tent
  The main crowd is outside the tent. Right now it's just you and Teisha in
  here.
end phrase

## The description with its `[if the Wandering Mercenaries are in the
## location]` sentence in place — a detail line would append it after the
## last sentence, so the whole text is carried again with the sentence where
## Gentry put it.

define phrase shoppers-wary-of-mercenaries
  The crowd is thick with shoppers and merchants alike, all shouting at each
  other, shuffling and squeezing against each other to get a better look at
  the merchandise. Some of them try to give the mercenaries a slightly wider
  berth, but elsewhere it's shoulder to shoulder. Staying out of sight should
  be easy, as long as you keep moving.
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-no-chat, stopping
  Some of the marketfolk glance at you; a few put their hands protectively
  around their moneypurses. But no one seems interested in having a
  conversation with a common street urchin.
or
  It's no use; these people just want to get on with their business and be
  left alone.
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-shove, randomly
  'Here, you! Scamper off, why don't you?'
or
  The shopper roughly shoulders you aside.
or
  The shopper waves you off, irritated.
or
  'Take it somewhere else, kid, before I call the guard.'
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-smell
  This many bodies, pressed this close together on a morning this hot, is not
  exactly the most pleasant thing your nose has encountered.
end phrase

define phrase shoppers-sound
  With dozens of people around you constantly chattering, muttering, arguing
  and haggling, it is difficult to make out anything specific.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE MARKET WALL (`story.ni:3461-3474`) — the perimeter's nine rooms.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the market wall
  aka pale stone wall, stone wall, market walls, walls, wall, tall wall, smooth wall, high wall
  scenery
  in the Northwest Junction

  The walls surrounding Grubber's are high and made of smooth, pale stone.

  when Jack moves, while market-perimeter
    move the market wall here
  end when

  on the player climbing
    refuse wall-too-high
  end on

define phrase wall-too-high
  The walls are too high, and you cannot get a handhold.
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE STALLS (`story.ni:3476-3525`) — the generic stall, everywhere in
## Grubber's. Entering one under a stallkeeper's eye is refused; elsewhere
## it is the platform's own can't-enter line, as the source fell to Inform's.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the stalls
  aka stall, stand, stands, other stalls, other stands, sellers, vendors, dealers
  scenery, plural
  in the Northwest Junction

  The stalls are simple constructions of wood and canvas.

  when Jack moves, while within-grubbers
    move the stalls here
  end when

  on the player examining
    phrase stalls-from-above when the player is in the Top of the Post or the player is in the Outer Market Roof
    phrase stalls-from-junction when the player is in the Northwest Junction or the player is in the Eastern Junction
    phrase stalls-outside-tent when the player is in Outside the Silk Tent
    phrase stalls-inside-tent when the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    phrase stalls-from-back when the player is in the Base of the Center Post
    phrase stalls-from-alley when the player is in the Alley
  end on

  on the player entering
    refuse when at-a-stall: stall-customers-outside
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse stalls-too-heavy
  end on

define phrase stalls-from-above
  The stalls are spread throughout the market below.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-from-junction
  This is a relatively clear part of the market; most of the stalls are deeper
  in.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-outside-tent
  This part of the market is reserved for Teisha's tent. Most of the stalls
  are elsewhere.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-inside-tent
  There aren't any stalls here inside Teisha's tent.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-from-back
  You can't really see much of the stalls from back here behind them.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-from-alley
  There aren't any stalls back here in the alley.
end phrase

define phrase stall-customers-outside
  The stallkeeper shoos you away. 'Customers on the outside!' he shouts.
end phrase

define phrase stalls-too-heavy
  The stalls are too heavy to tip over.
end phrase

